from pathlib import Path
import numpy as np,json
import matplotlib
matplotlib.use('Agg')
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
O=Path(__file__).parent;rows=json.loads((O/'balanced-body-comparison.json').read_text());f,axs=plt.subplots(1,3,figsize=(14,4.5));x=np.arange(len(rows));labels=[('HR' if r['day'].endswith('03') else 'TL')+' '+['<15°','15–30°','≥30°'][r['tier']]+'\nn='+str(r['n']) for r in rows]
for ax,key,title in zip(axs,['bank_rmse_deg','heading_final_error_deg','velocity_rmse_m_s'],['Median bank RMS error (deg)','Median absolute final heading error (deg)','Median velocity RMS error (m/s)']):
 ax.bar(x-.18,[r[key]['old_median'] for r in rows],width=.36,label='13-segment fit');ax.bar(x+.18,[r[key]['new_median'] for r in rows],width=.36,label='Balanced 62-segment fit');ax.set_xticks(x,labels,rotation=25,ha='right');ax.set_title(title,fontsize=10);ax.grid(axis='y',alpha=.2)
axs[0].legend(fontsize=8);f.suptitle('Broader training improves ordinary flight, but high-bank errors persist');f.tight_layout();f.savefig(O/'iteration-balanced-body-checks.png',dpi=160)
p=O/'physics-development.html';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig');s=s.replace('</html>','''<section id="balanced-physics-training"><h2>Correcting a gap in the physics-model training coverage</h2><p>The most recent two-body fits used 13 three-second segments. None exceeded 30 degrees of bank. The previously assembled broad candidate set contains 316 segments, including 18 above 30 degrees: two in Hood River and sixteen in Trout Lake. This made the narrow fit a poor basis for judging extrapolation into sustained steep turns.</p><figure><img src="iteration-training-coverage.png" alt="Counts of narrow and broad training candidates by flight and maximum bank angle"><figcaption>These counts describe the candidate sets before selecting the balanced training subset. All existing development and quarantine exclusions are retained.</figcaption></figure><p>A deterministic selection takes up to twelve evenly spaced segments from each flight/bank bin (&lt;15, 15–30, and ≥30 degrees), producing 62 training segments. Equal total weight per bin prevents ordinary Trout Lake autopilot flight from overwhelming the fit. The other 254 broad-set segments are excluded from this fit and used for internal comparison. They are not pristine final validation: earlier research models have already used this broad dataset.</p><figure><img src="iteration-balanced-body-checks.png" alt="Matched comparison of narrow and broad physics-model fit errors across flight and bank bins"><figcaption>Both parameter sets are evaluated on the same excluded segments, with the same causal initialization. Each bar shows a median across segments. There are no remaining high-bank Hood River samples in this comparison because both broad-set candidates entered training; the separately excluded Hood River development turns remain important stress cases.</figcaption></figure><p>Ordinary-flight performance improves. For the 199 excluded Trout Lake segments below 15 degrees, median bank RMS error drops from 3.16 to 2.42 degrees; median absolute final heading error from 12.50 to 9.30 degrees; and median velocity RMS error from 0.657 to 0.516 m/s. Hood River moderate-bank median bank error improves from 8.96 to 6.42 degrees. However, the four excluded Trout Lake high-bank segments worsen from 10.15 to 17.35 degrees median bank error, and the principal tight-turn development cases still fail. Thus broader coverage improves ordinary-flight calibration but does not fix this model's high-bank response.</p><p>An inner-solver precision audit also checked the inferred initial canopy angle. Tightening its scalar solver tolerance from 10⁻⁵ to 10⁻¹⁰ changes the normalized residual by at most 0.00025; checked parameter derivatives differ by less than 0.24% at the fitting step size. This numerical precision is not a plausible explanation for the large turn errors, so another precision-only refit is not warranted.</p><p>Evidence: <code>training-bank-coverage.json</code>, <code>balanced-body-fit.json</code>, <code>narrow-body-broad-evaluation.json</code>, <code>balanced-body-comparison.json</code>, <code>inner-alpha-precision-audit.json</code>. Reproduction: <code>audit_training_coverage.py</code>, <code>fit_balanced_body.py</code>, <code>evaluate_narrow_body_broad.py</code>, <code>audit_inner_alpha_precision.py</code>. This remains a research candidate, not an accepted simulator.</p></section></html>''');p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
